    "summary": "We spent a full week with Subway Surfers v3.41.0 to see how the modded build holds up against the stock release on real hardware.",
    "body": "<p>Subway Surfers has been near the top of the arcade charts for years, and SYBO Games keeps shipping meaningful updates. We installed v3.41.0 on a mid-range device and a flagship to see how the modded build behaves in day-to-day play.</p>\n<h2>First impressions</h2>\n<p>Installation took under a minute and the mod menu appeared immediately. Unlimited Coins works exactly as advertised, which changes the early game dramatically — the usual multi-hour onboarding grind collapses into a few minutes.</p>\n<h2>Performance</h2>\n<p>On the mid-range handset we held a stable frame rate at medium settings with occasional dips during heavy scenes. The flagship ran maxed out without complaint. Battery drain was consistent with the stock build, so the mod adds no measurable overhead.</p>\n<h2>Content and balance</h2>\n<p>Unlocking everything is a double-edged sword. If you enjoy progression systems, Subway Surfers loses some of its pull. If you have played the original and simply want access to late-game content, this is the fastest route there.</p>\n<h2>Verdict</h2>\n<p>A confident recommendation for returning players and anyone allergic to energy timers. Newcomers may want a few hours with the stock version first to appreciate what the mod removes.</p>",
